.TH CXL-FMD 8 "November 2026" "cxl-fmd 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
cxl-fmd \- CXL Fabric Manager daemon with a Redfish API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cxl-fmd
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-m ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-N
.IR threads ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-mctp\-restricted ]
.RB [ \-v ...]
.br
.B cxl-fmd
.B \-M
.I file
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-N
.IR threads ]
.RB [ \-v ...]
.br
.B cxl-fmd
.BR \-h " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cxl-fmd
is a Fabric Manager for Compute Express Link (CXL) memory fabrics.
It discovers CXL switches and Type 3 memory devices by sending them Component
Command Interface (CCI) commands, including the Fabric Management API, through
.BR libcxlmi ,
and serves what it finds, and the means to change it, as a DMTF Redfish
service over HTTP.
.PP
It represents switches with their ports, Virtual CXL Switches (VCSs) and
vPPBs; Single Logical Devices (SLDs), Multi-Logical Devices (MLDs) with their
Logical Devices, and Multi-Headed devices; the hosts on each VCS; and dynamic
capacity regions and extents.
A client provisions memory by creating and removing Redfish resources:
creating a connection between a host and a device binds a switch port or a
Logical Device to a vPPB of the host's VCS, and deleting it unbinds the vPPB;
memory chunks set Logical Device allocations, and actions add and release
dynamic capacity.
Each vPPB found bound to a memory device is a connection, whoever bound it:
.BR cxl-fmd ,
the switch's own configuration, another Fabric Manager, or another
interface of the switch.
.PP
The service root is at
.IR http://host:port/redfish/v1 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p\  port \fR,\ \fB\-\-port\  port
Listen for HTTP requests on
.IR port ,
from 1 to 65535.
The default is 8080.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mctp
Look for devices on MCTP, through the
.BR mctpd (8)
D-Bus service.
This is the default when neither
.B \-m
nor
.B \-i
is given.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ioctl
Look for devices through the kernel's CXL mailbox character devices in
.IR /dev/cxl .
May be combined with
.BR \-m ;
a device reached both ways is one component.
.TP
.BI \-M\  file \fR,\ \fB\-\-mock\  file
Serve the fabric described in the JSON topology
.I file
and send no commands to hardware.
See
.B MOCK MODE
below.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-check
Check that the topology is a valid CXL fabric, report, and exit without
serving it.
With
.B \-M
this checks a topology file.
The exit status is 0 if the topology is valid.
.TP
.BI \-e\  secs \fR,\ \fB\-\-event\-poll\  secs
Read the event logs every
.I secs
seconds: the Informational, Warning, Failure and Fatal logs of each switch and
memory device whose logs are the FM's, and the Dynamic Capacity event log of
each dynamic capacity device.
Records are cleared as they are read, and served as Redfish log entries.
The default is 10; 0 disables it.
The logs are read once when
.B cxl-fmd
starts, and those of a component when it sends an MCTP Event Notification,
which switches and memory devices on MCTP endpoints of their own are asked to
send.
.TP
.BI \-r\  secs \fR,\ \fB\-\-rescan\  secs
Look at the whole fabric again every
.I secs
seconds, from 0 to 86400.
The default is 30; 0 disables it.
See
.B KEEPING UP WITH THE FABRIC
below.
.TP
.BI \-N\  threads \fR,\ \fB\-\-threads\  threads
Serve requests on
.I threads
threads, from 1 to 256.
The default is 8.
This is how many requests can be in hand at once.
Requests that only read (GET) run together; a request that changes the fabric
runs alone.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-mctp\-restricted
Send the memory device commands that CXL r4.0 Table 8-308 marks O4 for MCTP
as MCTP messages, tunnelled ones too: Set Partition Info, Scan Media, and the
security commands (Set and Disable Passphrase, Freeze Security State,
Passphrase Secure Erase).
Note 4 of that table reads: \(lqSystems capable of management from Mailbox
registers and an MCTP-based CCI shall ensure that these commands are not
issued as MCTP messages while a device's mailboxes are operational.\(rq
Nothing the fabric manager can read over MCTP says whether they are.
Without this option, a request that would send one of these commands to a
device on MCTP, or through a tunnel, is refused with 409 and its action is
not advertised; only an SLD reached through the kernel's mailbox
.RB ( \-i )
is sent them.
The devices of a mock topology are taken as on MCTP.
Give it only when no host drives the devices.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log more.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH MOCK MODE
With
.B \-M
.B cxl-fmd
builds its topology from a JSON file and serves that.
Nothing is discovered and no commands are sent, so it needs no hardware and
no privileges; it is meant for developing and testing clients.
.PP
A topology file is read strictly, and the topology is then checked against the
CXL specification: what is bound to what, what a port sees of the device on
it, capacities, Logical Device counts, the heads of a Multi-Headed device,
dynamic capacity regions and extents.
A file that cannot be read, or a topology that could not be a real fabric, is
refused with the rule it breaks, and
.B cxl-fmd
exits.
.PP
The topologies that ship with
.BR cxl-fmd ,
and the format, are described in
.I mock/README.md
and
.I docs/mock-topology.md
in the source.
.PP
A topology can name devices to be pulled out of their switch ports, in its
.I unplug
list: each
.B SIGUSR1
pulls out the next one, and the mock switch reports it as a switch would.
Its
.I external
list holds binds and unbinds made outside
.BR cxl-fmd :
each
.B SIGUSR2
makes the next one.
.SH KEEPING UP WITH THE FABRIC
The fabric is discovered when
.B cxl-fmd
starts.
After that, on hardware:
.IP \(bu 2
The event logs of each switch are read and cleared.
A port that a Physical Switch Event Record names, for a change of its link
state or slot status, is read again: a device that has appeared on it is
discovered, and one that is no longer there is removed.
.IP \(bu 2
A Virtual CXL Switch Event Record of a binding change that no bind or unbind
of
.B cxl-fmd
explains has the switch's virtual switches read again, so that a binding made
elsewhere becomes, or stops being, a connection.
The virtual switches of a switch are also read before each request that binds
or unbinds on it.
.IP \(bu 2
Every
.B \-\-rescan
seconds the switches are read again in full and new endpoints are looked for,
including any that was not ready before.
A binding made elsewhere that the switch logged nothing for is seen then.
.IP \(bu 2
Every
.B \-\-rescan
seconds the memory size and LD allocations of each multi-logical and
multi-headed device found before are read again, and its memory domain and
chunks made what they say: an allocation made or changed elsewhere is seen
then.
The allocation of an LD is also read before each memory chunk of it is made
or deleted.
.IP \(bu 2
The Dynamic Capacity event log of each dynamic capacity device is read, and
the extents of the hosts it names are brought up to date.
.PP
A device on a switch port is removed when the port reports no device, or a
device of another kind, in its place: with its logical devices, memory domains
and chunks.
That rule is
.BR cxl-fmd 's;
the CXL specification does not say what a port reports after a removal.
A connection to it goes once its vPPB is read as unbound, as the switch
unbinds the logical devices of an MLD removed by surprise (CXL r4.0 Section
7.6.6.8).
One whose vPPB stays bound stands with its state Absent until it is deleted;
that the vPPB of an SLD's port stays bound is
.BR cxl-fmd 's
assumption, as the specification says only that the FM \(lqcan be notified
but is not involved\(rq (Section 7.6.6.7).
The managed hot-removal of an MLD, in which the FM unbinds each logical
device as its host releases it (Section 7.6.6.7), is not carried out.
A port whose link is down and that still reports its device keeps it.
A device attached directly, on an MCTP endpoint of its own, and a Multi-Headed
device, are not removed: they stay until
.B cxl-fmd
is restarted.
.SH EVENT LOGS
The event records read from a component are cleared from it, so that it can
record more, and kept by
.BR cxl-fmd ,
which serves them as Redfish LogEntry resources: those of a switch in
.IR /redfish/v1/Chassis/ switch /LogServices/EventLog ,
those of a memory device in the
.I DeviceLog
of the CXL logical device they are about.
.PP
Only logs that
.B cxl-fmd
takes to be the Fabric Manager's are read: those of a component's own MCTP
interface, and of the FM-owned logical device of an MLD behind a switch,
to which the FM tunnels its commands (CXL r4.0 Section 7.6.3.1).
That the FM-owned logical device's logs are the FM's to read and clear is
.BR cxl-fmd 's
inference: the specification says that the error messages it generates
\(lqmust only be routed to the FM\(rq (Section 2.4), not who reads its
event logs.
The logs of a Multi-Headed device are not read.
A component reached through the kernel's mailbox
.RB ( \-i )
shares its event logs with the kernel's driver, and they are left to it.
.SH BINDING IN THE BACKGROUND
A switch carries out a bind or an unbind as a background command.
When it has started one and not finished it,
.B cxl-fmd
answers the request with
.B 202 Accepted
and a Redfish task.
The connection is listed meanwhile with the state Starting while it is bound,
or Updating while it is unbound; a bind that fails leaves none, and an unbind
removes it once the vPPB reads unbound.
A request to bind the same target for the same host while that runs is
answered with the same task.
The
.I Location
header of the answer is a task monitor; a GET of it answers 202 while the
operation runs, and then with the outcome.
A switch runs one such command at a time: a request for another is answered
with
.B 503
and is to be tried again.
An unbind waits for the host for as long as the switch does;
.B cxl-fmd
sets no time limit.
The connection's
.I Cxl.ForceUnbind
action, which is
.BR cxl-fmd 's
own, sends the unbind again as a simulated surprise hot-removal.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Stop serving and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
In mock mode, pull the next device of the topology's
.I unplug
list out of its switch port.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
In mock mode, make the next bind or unbind of the topology's
.I external
list, as one made by another Fabric Manager would be.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped by a signal, or with
.B \-c
the topology is valid, or
.B \-h
or
.B \-V
was given.
.TP
.B 1
An option is invalid, the topology file could not be read or is not a valid
fabric, discovery failed, or the HTTP port could not be opened.
.SH SECURITY
.B cxl-fmd
serves plain HTTP on every interface and does not authenticate clients.
Anything that can reach the port can reconfigure the fabric.
Run it on a management network, or behind a reverse proxy that provides TLS
and authentication.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve the devices found on MCTP on port 8080:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cxl-fmd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Serve devices found on MCTP and through the kernel, on port 8443, with eight
times as many threads and more logging:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cxl-fmd \-m \-i \-p 8443 \-N 64 \-v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a mock topology:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cxl-fmd \-\-mock mock/large-fabric.json \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Serve it, and list the switches:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cxl-fmd \-\-mock mock/large-fabric.json &
curl http://localhost:8080/redfish/v1/Fabrics/CXL/Switches
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mctpd (8)
.PP
Compute Express Link Specification; DMTF DSP0266, Redfish Specification;
DMTF DSP0288, CXL to Redfish Mapping Specification.
.PP
.I docs/Redfish-API.md
in the source lists the resources served and the CXL commands behind them.
